Title: "Let"
Id: "1a34321a-e685-48f6-92fa-db20cdf46b16"

Text: "`let:` introduces one or more local bindings in a child context, assumes an
optional `where:` guard, and checks `then:` with those bindings and assumptions
available.

```mlg-fragment
[LabelHeader]?
let: <BindingOrSpecUnion>
where?: <ClauseUnion>+
then: <ClauseUnion>+
```

```mlg-fragment
let: n \"in\" X
where: n != x
then: n = n
```

It has the same shape as `forAll:`, but records a local naming rather than
universal quantification. `where:` is optional; `then:` is required.

`let:` and `where:` are binding positions, so bare `is` and `\:...:/`
specifications may introduce symbols there. `then:` is a statement position, so
use the predicate forms `is?`, `is_not?`, and `\:...?:/`.

Bindings introduced by `let:` are local to the clause group that introduces
them."
Id: "1b3520b6-ca0b-48a1-a21c-c147f6361512"
